[Music]  this is a story about ireland and it's a  story about an event that you've  probably heard of  but might not know much about  it's usually called the irish potato  famine  in most history books the irish potato  famine doesn't get a lot of attention  at most it appears as a pretty simple  story to explain irish migration abroad  in the beginning  irish people depended on the potato for  food  then there was a potato disease  many irish died and others migrated  abroad on what they called coffin ships  to england scotland australia and north  america but the story of the famine is a  lot more complicated and a lot more  disturbing than that it was much less  natural and much more artificial than  you might think  and it all goes back to a man named  thomas malthus  malthus was a british economist and in  1798 malthus put forward an influential  idea and a rather counterintuitive one  malthus said that when food production  increased people were better off for a  little but that improvement led to an  increase in population which eventually  caused societal problems  there were too many people and not  enough food to go around  poverty and famine with the natural  results  like animals malthus said  humans had a certain carrying capacity  when it exceeded that carrying capacity  enough people would die to return the  population to a natural equilibrium  so in the 19th century  that so-called malthusian model became  the conventional wisdom  in european intellectuals  it led them to focus on what they called  overpopulation and made them think of  famine as nature reducing population to  a sustainable level  [Music]  no serious economist today will tell you  that having too many people is the  reason people are poor  or hungry  in 1981 the economist amartya sen  explained the real cause of most  historical famines  if you look at the historical record sen  said  a collective lack of food is never the  real problem the cause of famines while  it often has something to do with a  decline in agricultural production  is more related to the unequal  distribution of food  there's enough food for everyone but  some people can exchange what they have  for that food  sen found that famine is usually not the  result of too little food  but of exploitation and unequal economic  relations  irish people were the other against whom  the british could define themselves they  were viewed as lazy barbaric barely  human  ireland itself  was run as a colony for the emerging  class of british capitalists  while england was rapidly becoming the  wealthiest country in the world  ireland was kept incredibly poor  the population was almost entirely  composed of peasants  the large majority of all houses were  huts  made of mud  and the land those peasants lived on was  owned almost entirely by absentee  english landlords who never visited  ireland and extracted exorbitant amounts  in rent even the british government  regarded these landlords with disdain  calling them quote  the most oppressive species of tyrant  that has ever lent assistance to the  destruction of a country  so most irish people were on a treadmill  of extreme poverty  they were the most destitute people in  all of europe  in many districts one government report  found  their only food is the potato  they're only beverage water  a bed or blanket is a rare luxury  and that part about the potato is  especially important  ireland has a lot of fertile soil and a  topography that is ideal for grazing  cattle  but when the british came in and  colonized ireland they systematically  took that land and used it to produce  beef for the domestic english market  and so the economically poor irish  turned to the potato  a versatile crop that could be grown in  large numbers on bad soil  and while the potato was adaptable it  was also really unreliable there had  been widespread potato failures pretty  much every other year in the 19th  century  and in the 1840s  a new potato disease originally from  north america arrived in europe  huge potato crop failures sprung up in  northern france in belgium and in the  netherlands  but none of those places were as  dependent on the potato as a source of  food as was ireland  so when the disease got to ireland in  the 1840s  the results  were devastating  in 1845 about a third of the irish crop  was destroyed  by 1846  75 of it was gone  hunger spread rapidly  with unprecedented severity  this period became known in ireland as  andrajil the bad life  but as amartya sen observed this wasn't  just about a pure lack of food  in fact during the entire period of the  famine ireland was continuing to export  food out of the country enough cows  sheep pigs and grain were being shipped  out of ireland that could feed the irish  population twice over  but the british government refused to do  anything about it  soon the irish began begging their  british overlords for relief and early  on the conservative party government  then the representatives of the old  british aristocracy had a fairly  sympathetic response  they bought food from america and  brought it to ireland distributing it to  the poor  but in 1846 a different party the whigs  came to power in britain and the whigs  did not have the sort of aristocratic  paternalism that the conservatives had  they were advocates of a philosophy  called laissez faire  the idea that the free market left alone  would solve virtually any problem  so the person they put in charge of  famine relief in ireland was a man named  charles trevelyan  trevelyan had been a colonial  administrator in india and he was a big  believer in those mathusian ideas about  population he didn't really want to stop  the starvation  he thought it was well  necessary  the famine trevelyan said was quote an  effective mechanism for reducing surplus  population  he said  it was the judgment of god that sent the  famine to teach the irish a lesson  the real evil he said  is not the physical evil of the famine  but the moral evil of the selfish  perverse and turbulent character  of the irish people  so trevelyan's solution to the famine  was to let it ride  the laissez-faire wigs decided to change  nothing  they refused to stop the movement of  food to england and limited the amount  of food they offered to the starving  irish people  the food that was being imported into  ireland was largely used not to feed the  irish  but to feed the cattle being slaughtered  for british consumption  even when they did offer some relief to  the irish peasants  they instituted moralistic tests  requiring that recipients live as  indentured servants in a workhouse to  improve their work ethic the british  even required that food only go to  people who owned almost no land  forcing impoverished farmers to sell all  the remaining land they had to their  landlords many of whom  lived in britain  and so  the famine only got worse  diseases of hunger like typhus dysentery  and cholera spread wildly killing just  as many people as the lack of food  social structures broke down  ordinary people turned in mass to  begging crime prostitution in western  ireland some resorted to cannibalism the  british fearing a rebellion  sent army troops instead of food  as the news of the famine spread all  over the world  shiploads of food finally began arriving  americans from all strata generously  sent money and food over the atlantic  only to have their donations met with  huge british cargo fees upon arrival  even the choctaw people donated to the  famine relief funds just a few years  after they had experienced starvation  themselves on the trail of tears it was  a beautiful example of international  solidarity but these donations weren't  enough to stop the ruthless exploitation  by british landlords even when enough  food was being imported  it wasn't getting to the irish people  starvation and death continued  impoverished irish with barely enough to  eat  were evicted from their homes  one priest remembers the scene of a mass  eviction  several hundred human beings were driven  from their homes in one day and sent  adrift on the world  to gratify the caprice of one who before  god and man  probably deserved less consideration  than the last and the least of them  the hard scenes i had then witnessed i  must remember all my life long  the wailing of women the screams the  terror the consternation of children  the speechless agony of honest  industrious men  rung tears of grief from all who saw  them  by the time the famine began to end in  the early 1850s about 20 percent of the  irish population  2 million human beings had died of  disease or hunger  another 2 million had left for brighter  shores  today ireland's population is still  lower than it was in the 1840s  but ireland has never forgotten the  famine and the generosity that some  showed to them  during covet when the people of the  navajo nation were hit hard  ireland repaid their kindness shown to  them during the famine by raising 2  million dollars  and ireland has never forgotten those  who did not help them  the british government and all those who  valued profits  over humanity  as the british historian ajp taylor  would later write  the english governing class had the  blood of two million irish people on  their hands  all  ireland  was a belsen  i'm rory duffy  professor of history and filmmaker for  the gravel institute  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you

